/// Threads that run alongside the rest of the program, and the queues they talk through (D121).
///
/// `parallel for` is how Calx does work faster: it gives the same result on any number of
/// threads (D21). A thread here is for something else, such as a service that waits on the
/// network or the disk while the program goes on. What it does when depends on timing, so
/// starting one takes `unsafe`, as reading the clock does.
///
/// A thread runs a task: a value whose type implements `Task`, which moves to the thread. The
/// thread calls the task's `run` and ends when `run` returns. `Thread.spawn(task)` starts it,
/// and `join()` waits for it to end. A thread that's dropped without `join()` carries on by
/// itself.
///
/// Threads share no memory: each value has one owner (D76). They talk through queues instead.
/// `Receiver<T>()` makes a queue of messages of type `T`, which has to be `Send`, and is the end
/// that takes them. `sender()` makes an end that sends. Each message moves into the queue, and
/// comes out at the other end in the order it was sent.
///
/// - `s.send(m)` moves `m` into the queue. If every `Receiver` is gone, `m` is dropped.
/// - `r.next()` takes the first message, or gives `none` at once if there isn't one.
/// - `r.wait()` waits for a message, and gives `none` once the queue is empty and every
///   `Sender` is gone.
/// - `share()` makes another end of the same queue, and `close()` drops one now.
///
/// A queue can't be used, and a thread can't be joined, inside a `parallel for`.
module std.thread

/// What a thread does. `run` takes the task, which the thread owns from then on.
pub interface Task {
    fn run(own self)
}
